a lot of you guys have been asking us to  take the new 2020 $1,800 MacBook Pro and  combine it with an eg pu and then  compare that to the base 16 inch MacBook  Pro by itself to see which setup is more  powerful for things like video editing  and other performance tasks so we did  just that and we found some results that  you probably aren't expecting but before  we get into those performance results I  want to first show you guys the  enclosure in the graphics card that we  chose for this build and why we  recommend those parts the razor core X  for $300 has been the most reliable in  our experience so it's a great choice  and you also have the option of  upgrading to the chroma model which  features RGB lighting and extra ports  for connecting things like a mouse and  keyboard as for the graphics card We  strongly recommend buying the 5700 XT  for 380 dollars it is by far the best  choice and we've proven that when we  compared it to other popular cards and  not only that but Apple has added a new  W 5700 X graphics option for their 2019  Mac Pro workstation which will be  shipping very soon the reason this  matters is because it's essentially  identical to the 5700 XT graphics card  that we recommend except for the vram  difference so this means the Apple is  going to be releasing new drivers for  both of these cards so the 5700 XT is  going to be getting faster over time so  with this a GPU the total price of the  13-inch MacBook Pro EGP you setup  becomes two thousand four hundred and  eighty dollars which is actually more  expensive than the base 16 ish MacBook  Pro but the benefit is that you get  great portability for when you're on the  go and then a big boost and graphics  power while plugged into the each of you  at home now before we get into video  gaming 3d rendering and video editing  performance you need to know the most  important thing about using the e GPU  setup you absolutely must use it within  external display if you want to get the  most power out of it and on top of that  we add  she noticed that plugging the display  into certain outputs on the graphics  card gave us better performance so  you'll have to experiment with this and  test out the different outputs to see  which one gives you the best performance  so just to give you guys an idea of the  performance difference the 13-inch  MacBook Pro by itself scored ten  thousand a hundred and sixty six points  in Geekbench 5s metal graphics test  which is decent for a 13-inch laptop but  with the EGP you set up plugged into an  external monitor that score went up to  thirty one thousand a hundred and  twenty-two tripling the graphics  performance however this is actually a  lower score than what this card is  supposed to get and we think it's  because of a driver issue that should  hopefully get fixed when Apple updates  the drivers for the Mac Pro's w50 700 X  graphics card but luckily we discovered  a method to work around this driver  issue by connecting two displays at once  to the e GPU and with that we got a  better score of over 53,000 and there's  a chance that it could get even better  with three displays now you're probably  thinking there's no way I'm gonna use  two displays with my MacBook Pro and the  good news is that you don't have to you  can actually buy a couple of these cheap  HDMI dummy adapters which trick your a  GPU into thinking that their actual  displays and then you get those extra  performance gains without having to buy  expensive monitors well leave some links  down to those dummy adapters in the  description so without further ado let's  first get into video gaming performance  starting with the classic Unigine Heaven  gaming benchmark on the extreme preset  the MacBook Pro alone only got 13 and a  half FPS in this test but with the e GPU  that score shot up to 70 FPS completely  blowing away the 16 inch MacBook Pro in  terms of gaming performance so then we  played some fortnight to see which  option worked better using the  recommended settings that you see right  here and we saw that the e GPU set up  completely blew the 16 inch MacBook Pro  away  so we then adjusted the settings to  1440p resolution but turned off a lot of  the extra graphics settings and the 16  inch MacBook Pro barely improved so you  can see that the e GPU is by far  the best choice for gaming next up we  tested blenders quick graphics benchmark  in the e GPU setup  wiped the floor with a 16 inch MacBook  Pro mostly because the 5700 XD graphics  card has 8 gigabytes of video memory so  for graphics rendering the e GPU is  definitely the better choice and now  let's get into video editing starting  with the graphics heavy Bruce X  benchmark in Final Cut Pro 10 here we  can see that the e GPU setup is actually  almost twice as fast as the base 16 inch  Pro and it made a huge difference  compared to the 13-inch Pro by itself  and for our 22nd 4k stabilization test  the EGP you helped the 13-inch match up  to the 16 so we can see that it's really  helping for these graphics heavy tests  however when we start to move over to  our exporting tests like this  five-minute 4k h.264 clip which is the  codec that most youtubers use the 16  inch MacBook Pro finished quite a lot  faster even though the e GPU did help  the 13 inch MacBook Pro finish almost  twice as fast now the reason the 16 inch  is faster is that when you're exporting  videos the data has to go from the CPU  to the each of you for graphics  processing and then back to the CPU and  all of that has to happen within a  single cable which has limited bandwidth  but with the 16 inch MacBook Pro the  dedicated graphics chip is connected to  the CPU by incredibly fast PCI Express  Lanes with less latency and much more  bandwidth so what finishes the export  faster even though the graphics  performance is lower and because of that  we see very similar results in the Canon  C 200 raw  60fps export test with the 16 inch  MacBook Pro winning once again now in  terms of timeline smoothness the 16 inch  wins again with close to the full 60fps  of the clip but for the 13-inch MacBook  Pro you don't get a very smooth  experience even with the each of you  helping it out a bit so as we can see  for video editing within Final Cut Pro  10 the e GPU definitely helps out but  the 16 inch MacBook Pro is still the  best choice especially for exporting  videos  now we also tested black magic resolve  studio and we actually got some very  different results we ran the same  20-second 4k stabilization test and we  can see that our times got a bit slower  all around and that's because Final Cut  Pro is the king of stabilization but one  interesting difference is that the e GPU  was the winner by far and that's because  resolve studio is actually more  optimized for each GPUs than Final Cut  Pro currently is and now running that  same 5-minute 4k export test we can see  that the speeds got a bit quicker but  the sixteen inch MacBook Pro is still  the clear winner because of those  bandwidth limitations with the HBU now  by far the biggest difference we saw  with the e GPU was in the Blackmagic Raw  test exporting this clip took over an  hour on the 13-inch MacBook Pro by  itself but with the help of the e GPU it  took only four minutes in 25 seconds  but surprisingly the 16 inch MacBook Pro  still finished quicker but the more  important difference is the timeline  smoothness the 13-inch Pro can only play  this back at three frames per second  which is unbearable if you're trying to  edit video but with the e GPU it plays  back at perfectly smooth 24 fps just  like the 16 inch MacBook Pro now before  I get into my conclusion for this video  I do want to mention that in a GPU is  gonna do absolutely nothing for things  like programming photo editing and using  Logic Pro 10 so don't even think about  wasting your money if you do those  things on your macbook but from all of  the tests that we just ran we can  conclude that if you play games that  each APU is a must and honestly for  gaming you don't actually need the  $1,800 13-inch model the base $1300  model will perform close to the same and  we'll show you guys that when we test it  sometime next week so if you're not  already subscribed go ahead and do that  right now so you don't miss out we also  notice that for 3d rendering the e GPU  was once again the better choice since  it's so graphics heavy but for video  editing we can conclude that the 16 inch  MacBook Pro is by far the better choice  since it's not only faster in most of  the tests but it's cheaper  and you don't have to deal with  everything that comes with the ETP you  however if here's somebody who  absolutely must have the portability of  the 13-inch MacBook Pro or if you  already have a 13-inch then investing  about six hundred and eighty dollars  into the e GPU setup is in excellent  choice and it'll make a big difference  for video editing as well so if this  video helps you make a decision  we'll have links to each of the Mac  books and the easy bu parts down in the  description below so go ahead and tap  the like button and click the circle  above to see our 13-inch MacBook Pro  each of you video coming soon thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  one  [Music]

